OwnGit {{.Version}}
Portable archive for {{.Target}}

OwnGit keeps private Git repositories and a browser dashboard on a computer you
control. This archive is a portable build. Unpacking it does not register a
service, add anything to PATH, or write outside the directory you unpack it in.
Running it writes host-local state, described under Data below.

Requirements
  Git must be on PATH with an executable git-http-backend. OwnGit uses
  the installed Git for repository storage and Smart HTTP. Pull request
  merging needs Git 2.38 or newer. Older Git versions refuse the merge.

Start
  From this unpacked directory, run:
    {{.Invocation}} serve
  {{.Invocation}} is a relative invocation that works while your shell is in
  this unpacked directory. Unpacking adds nothing to PATH, so from anywhere
  else use the actual absolute path of the {{.Binary}} file in this
  directory. The same applies to every command in this note.

  On a computer with a screen, the dashboard listens on 127.0.0.1:7654.
  Without a screen, first start listens on every address for setup from
  another device. Until setup ends, only the setup page is available.

  First start from a terminal
    Setup runs in the terminal. Choose English or Korean, then one of:
    - "Continue in this terminal" to answer the setup questions there.
    - "Open the web dashboard" to finish setup in a browser. The browser
      shows a short code; approve the browser in the terminal only if the
      terminal shows the same code.

  First start without a terminal (for example as a background service)
    OwnGit writes an owner-readable setup file in the state directory and
    logs the file's path. In a session with a desktop that someone sees,
    it also opens the file in a browser, unless it was started with
    --no-open. Every OwnGit service starts with --no-open. Otherwise open
    the file in a browser on this computer yourself, or run
    {{.Invocation}} setup-link for a new setup link.

  To start automatically in the background, run:
    {{.Invocation}} service install

  After setup, open Settings, Storage & recovery, Backups. Choose a backup
  folder outside the state and repository folders and turn scheduled
  backups on. The first backup starts when you save.

{{if .IconApp}}Menu bar icon
  OwnGit.app is the OwnGit icon in the macOS menu bar. It shows whether
  OwnGit runs, the clone address, the latest pushes and Open dashboard.
  {{.Invocation}} service install opens it in your desktop login, and it
  then opens whenever you sign in. {{.Invocation}} tray off hides it, and
  {{.Invocation}} tray on shows it again; OwnGit keeps running either way.

  The app holds its own copy of {{.Binary}}, so you can move it to
  /Applications. Do this on macOS 27 if you use a menu bar manager such as
  Hidden Bar, which can hide the icon of an app outside /Applications.
  Then run {{.Invocation}} service install again. OwnGit uses the app in
  /Applications only when it has the same version as {{.Binary}};
  otherwise it uses OwnGit.app beside {{.Binary}}, if one is there.

{{end}}Data
  Host-local state lives in the platform configuration directory under
  "owngit", or in ~/.owngit when no configuration directory is available.
  Repositories live in the storage directory chosen during setup.

Update
  {{.Invocation}} update prints the one command that replaces this program
  with the newest release. OwnGit never runs it or updates itself.

Remove
  Run {{.Invocation}} uninstall to remove the service that "service install"
  registered. Then delete this directory. Repositories and state are kept
  unless you delete them yourself.{{if .IconApp}} If OwnGit used the app in
  /Applications, uninstall also says to move that app to the Trash.{{end}}

